ctors are additive, meaning that interactions are either negligible or can be estimated.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and homoscedastic (constant variance).\n3. The experimental runs are independent of each other.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nNonregular fractional factorial designs are applicable in situations where:\n- The number of factors is moderate to large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n- The primary goal is to screen for significant factors rather than to estimate all interactions.\n- Resources (time, budget, materials) are limited, necessitating fewer experimental runs.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data from nonregular fractional factorial designs typically involves:\n1. Conducting an analysis of variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the main effects and interactions.\n2. Using regression analysis to model the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n3. Employing graphical methods (e.g., interaction plots, main effects plots) to visualize the effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Efficient use of resources by reducing the number of experimental runs required.\n- Ability to identify important factors quickly, making it suitable for preliminary studies.\n- Flexibility in design allows for the exploration of a wide range of factors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Potential for confounding effects, particularly if higher-order interactions are not negligible.\n- Limited ability to estimate all interactions, which may lead to incomplete understanding of the system.\n- Requires careful selection of factors to avoid confounding and ensure valid conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nNonregular fractional factorial designs are related to:\n- Regular fractional factorial designs, which do not allow for confounding.\n- Plackett-Burman designs, which are also used for screening but focus on main effects only.\n- Response surface metho